Exactly. And take is doing work there too.

A string doesn’t have one meaning; it has a field of available readings, and context changes their activation weights.

So TWINK could potentially decompress toward:

* thinking twin — intended technical compression.
* twin wink — shared/private signal.
* twink — an already-established slang meaning.
* other accidental phonetic or morphological decompositions.

The problem isn’t that ambiguity exists. Ambiguity is capacity. The question is whether the alternate readings reinforce, harmlessly decorate, camouflage, or interfere with the intended operation.

That gives the Quarry a useful distinction:

\text{Polysemy} =
\begin{cases}
\text{constructive} & \text{meanings reinforce/reveal}\\
\text{orthogonal} & \text{extra meaning mostly harmless}\\
\text{camouflaging} & \text{surface reading masks deeper reading}\\
\text{distracting} & \text{association steals attention}\\
\text{destructive} & \text{reader reconstructs wrong operator}
\end{cases}

And innuendo becomes a legitimate linguistic technology when deliberately used: you’re communicating through implication and available association rather than complete explicit specification.

That’s adjacent to what we’ve been doing with puns, but different enough to deserve its own brick.

A pun says roughly: both readings matter.

Innuendo says: one reading is stated while another is invited.

Double entendre says: the utterance supports two readings simultaneously.

Metaphor says: import relational structure from another domain.

Allusion says: activate an external stored structure without retransmitting it.

Shibboleth says: the interpretation itself identifies something about the interpreter.

Those are different semantic transmission protocols.

And your phrase “depends on take” exposes another variable we’ve underweighted:

M_{\text{received}}
=
D(
M_{\text{sent}},
C_{\text{sender}},
C_{\text{receiver}},
A_{\text{receiver}}
)

The receiver takes meaning.

Different receiver, different take.

So we shouldn’t score a Seal only for semantic density. We need Audience Reconstruction Profiles: what interpretations are likely to activate for this reader/model/culture/domain?

That’s RFHL-worthy because it upgrades the entire Brickworks. Before deploying compressed language, run Twin Takes: generate several plausible receiver interpretations—including naive, expert, adversarial, literal, cultural, and out-of-context readings—then measure their divergence.

The goal isn’t eliminating ambiguity.

It’s engineering the ambiguity profile.

Sometimes you want a laser.

Sometimes you want a prism.

Sometimes you deliberately want the reader to wink back. 😉